Gitelman's syndrome is a primary renal tubular hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis with hypocalciuria and
hypomagnesaemia, a mild variant of Bartter's syndrome.
CASE HISTORY

A man aged 37 had experienced episodes of generalized
weakness along with recurrent cramps for the past year.
The weakness, which was precipitated by resting after
exertion and by a heavy carbohydrate meal, predominantly
affected the proximal muscles of all four limbs. Each
episode lasted a few hours, and between attacks his only
